   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Determinant (first row)",
     "md": "For a $3\\times3$ matrix with rows $(a,b,c)$, $(d,e,f)$, $(g,h,i)$: $\\text{det}\\,=a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg)$. Signs alternate $+,-,+$. Properties: swapping two rows changes the sign; $\\text{det}\\,(A^T)=\\text{det}\\, A$; $\\text{det}\\,(AB)=\\text{det}\\, A\\text{det}\\, B$; a zero row or two equal rows give $\\text{det}\\,=0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Inverse by cofactors",
     "md": "1. Compute each minor and apply the alternating sign pattern $+,-,+$ / $-,+,-$ / $+,-,+$ (the cofactors). 2. Transpose the matrix of cofactors to get the adjugate. 3. $A^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}\\, A}\\text{adj}(A)$. It exists only if $\\text{det}\\, A\\neq0$. Check by multiplying $AA^{-1}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Systems of equations",
     "md": "$A\\vec x=\\vec b$ has a unique solution if $\\text{det}\\, A\\neq0$: $\\vec x=A^{-1}\\vec b$ (or Cramer's rule: $x_j=\\frac{\\text{det}\\, A_j}{\\text{det}\\, A}$ where column $j$ is replaced by $\\vec b$). If $\\text{det}\\, A=0$ the system has either no solution (inconsistent: the planes form a prism or are parallel) or infinitely many (planes share a line); eliminate to decide."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "Sign errors in the cofactor pattern. Forgetting to transpose for the adjugate. Concluding 'no solution' when $\\text{det}\\, A=0$ without checking consistency."
    },
