   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Eigenvalues",
     "md": "A non-zero vector $\\vec v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if $A\\vec v=\\lambda\\vec v$: the transformation only stretches the vector (by $\\lambda$) without turning it. Eigenvalues satisfy the **characteristic equation** $\\text{det}\\,(A-\\lambda I)=0$; for $2\\times2$: $\\lambda^2-(\\text{trace})\\lambda+\\text{det}\\, A=0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Eigenvectors",
     "md": "For each $\\lambda$ solve $(A-\\lambda I)\\vec v=\\vec 0$; the two equations are multiples of each other, so you get a direction (any non-zero multiple works). Sum of eigenvalues $=$ trace, product $=\\text{det}\\, A$ (a useful check)."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "propriete",
     "title": "Diagonalisation and Cayley–Hamilton",
     "md": "If $P$ has the eigenvectors as columns and $D$ is diagonal with the eigenvalues, then $A=PDP^{-1}$ and $A^n=PD^nP^{-1}$. Cayley–Hamilton: $A$ satisfies its own characteristic equation, e.g. $A^2-(\\text{tr}A)A+(\\text{det}\\, A)I=0$, which gives $A^{-1}$ and $A^2$ quickly."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "The zero vector is NOT an eigenvector. Do not forget to check the trace and determinant. Write the eigenvectors as columns of $P$ in the same order as the eigenvalues in $D$."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $A=$ [[4, 1], [2, 3]].",
     "steps": [
      "Trace 7, determinant 10: $\\lambda^2-7\\lambda+10=0$, $\\lambda=5$ or $2$.",
      "$\\lambda=5$: $-x+y=0$, vector $(1,1)$. $\\lambda=2$: $2x+y=0$, vector $(1,-2)$. Check: $A(1,1)=(5,5)$; $A(1,-2)=(2,-4)$."
     ],
     "answer": "5 with $(1,1)$; 2 with $(1,-2)$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Show that $A=$ [[4, 1], [2, 3]] satisfies $A^2-7A+10I=0$ and deduce $A^{-1}$.",
     "steps": [
      "$A^2=$ [[18, 7], [14, 11]], $7A=$ [[28, 7], [14, 21]]; $A^2-7A+10I=$ [[0, 0], [0, 0]].",
      "Rearrange: $A(7I-A)=10I$, so $A^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}(7I-A)=\\frac{1}{10}$ [[3, −1], [−2, 4]]."
     ],
     "answer": "$A^{-1}=\\frac1{10}$ [[3, −1], [−2, 4]]",
